lse,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"**Why:** Under IRC §852(b)(6), a RIC recognizes no gain when distributing appreciated securities to meet redemptions. Because ETF redemptions run through authorized participants, the fund can deliver its lowest-basis lots, often via \"heartbeat\" trades, purging embedded gains without realizing them. A cash-dealing mutual fund must sell stock to pay redeemers. RICs effectively must distribute those net gains to avoid fund-level and excise tax, so remaining holders owe tax on gains they didn't choose to realize.\n\n**Limits:**\n- Mostly deferral: you pay capital-gains tax when you sell.\n- Dividends stay taxable annually.\n- Gains can still arise from cash redemptions, cash mergers, forced rebalancing, or index changes.\n- No benefit in tax-deferred accounts.\n- Results depend on flows and turnover.\n- Legislative risk: Congress has floated repealing §852(b)(6) for ETFs.\n- Step-up at death can eliminate deferred gain, depending on the estate.","ttft_ms":6735,"type":"result","duration_ms":8991,"uuid":"ecabfeea-0531-413c-9c12-fd7acf33343c","ttft_stream_ms":1142,"time_to_request_ms":71,"first_content_frame_ms":1142,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
